# TLS + routing for this instance. Every hostname below comes from the environment, so nothing here names
# your domain and this file needs no edit to move between boxes.
#
# ★★★ THIS BOX HAS SIX FACES AND pk34 IS WHERE IT LEARNED TO SAY SO. Three shops, two admins and the
# counter — and until that slice only three of them could be declared at all, because `FORGE_DOMAIN`,
# `FORGE_ADMIN_DOMAIN` and `FORGE_TOTEM_DOMAIN` are SINGULAR variables for a box with two brands, four
# stores and two admins. The outlet and the coffee shop had no variable; the second admin was a file
# somebody hand-wrote into `caddy/extra/` per box.
#
# ⛔ WHICH HOSTNAME IS WHICH IS NOT DECIDED HERE. `seed/box.json` declares one `domain` per store and one
# `admin_domain` per tenant — a hostname is DATA, it is the `host` column the kernel keys its directory on —
# and each declaration names the variable that carries it here. This file decides the other half: which
# CONTAINER answers at that hostname. `bin/box-domains.guard.mjs` grades the pairing in both directions, and
# grades the third end too: compose has to DELIVER each of these variables to this container.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND THAT THIRD END IS WHERE THIS FILE WAS ALREADY DEAD. Measured 2026-09-12 on the live bench:
# `docker inspect …-caddy-1` shows this container holding exactly THREE FORGE_* variables — FORGE_DOMAIN,
# FORGE_ADMIN_DOMAIN, FORGE_CONTROL_ALLOW_CIDR. `FORGE_TOTEM_DOMAIN` was never passed, so `{$FORGE_TOTEM_DOMAIN}`
# resolved to the EMPTY STRING, and an empty site address is not a missing host:
#
#     caddy validate → Error: adapting config using caddyfile: server block without any key is global
#                             configuration, and if used, it must be first
#
# This file is compose's DEFAULT (`${FORGE_CADDYFILE:-./caddy/Caddyfile}`), so the edge a deployment of this
# instance would have got was one that cannot load — store, checkout and admin down together. The bench never
# showed it because the bench opts into `caddy/Caddyfile.local`. Same species as A10, one layer out.
#
# ★ HENCE THE SENTINEL ON EVERY ADDRESS, AND IT IS MEASURED. `{$VAR:something.unset.localhost}`: with
# `caddy:2` v2.11.4 a `.localhost` name is issued by Caddy's OWN internal CA — `certificate obtained
# successfully … issuer:"local"`, 11 ms, no ACME request at all — so a variable nobody set costs ONE face on
# a name nothing resolves, and never the whole edge. The defaults are DISTINCT on purpose: two site blocks
# with one address is a duplicate site address, which is the dead edge again by another road.
#
# Caddy provisions a Let's Encrypt certificate for each real hostname automatically — which means the DNS
# records must already point at this box when you first bring the stack up, and ports 80 and 443 must be
# reachable.
#
# A SHOP HOSTNAME SERVES TWO FRONTS, SPLIT BY PATH. Most of it is the STOREFRONT — the vitrine, the half you
# are free to fork. `/checkout`, `/account`, `/.well-known/` and the checkout's own assets go to the CHECKOUT
# container: the part that handles money and sign-in, that we keep evolving, and that you do not have to
# maintain to own your storefront. Same hostname on purpose — a subdomain would put the cart and the session
# cookies on a different origin, and a shopper would lose their cart on the way to paying.
#
# The browser never reaches /v1/*: the fronts and the admin talk to the kernel server-side over the compose
# network. What IS public are the doors meant for callers that are not browsers — an ERP with an API key, an
# agent over MCP, the CLI:
#   /v1/*                — the port: public reads, the command face, the internal read face (the kernel
#                          authenticates every request; an anonymous call to an authenticated face is refused
#                          by the kernel, not by this file)
#   /webhooks/payment/*  — the payment provider POSTs here
#   /webhooks/channel/*  — a channel provider POSTs delivery receipts here (the address a channel app's
#                          ficha prints for you to paste into the provider's console)
#   /health              — liveness, for your monitoring
#
# And ONE door that is deliberately shut: /v1/control/*, the platform face. It carries cross-tenant power, so
# it answers 403 from the public interface unless you name a source range for it. The full map, and what to do
# if you want it reachable from your VPN, is in docs/conventions/surface-exposure.md.
#
# ⚠️ Coarse per-IP protection (an abuse cap in front of everything) is NOT in this file: the stock `caddy:2`
# image has no rate-limit module, and a directive this binary does not know would not degrade one route — it
# would stop the whole file loading, and your STORE with it. The per-credential cap is inside the kernel and
# is always on. The recipe for the edge layer, if you want it, is in the doc above.

# ★★★ ONE SHOP HOSTNAME, DEFINED ONCE. Every store of this box serves the SAME doors — that is not tidiness,
# it is the path split itself: a hostname that carries `/checkout*` and a sibling that does not is a shop
# whose buyer meets the other shop's 404 with a full cart. Copied by hand into three site blocks this is
# exactly the list that rots in silence (this house has paid for that: «cinco listas digitadas para mover um
# bloco», 09/09), so it is a snippet and each hostname passes the ONE thing that differs — the container its
# fall-through reaches. `bin/box-domains.guard.mjs` expands this the way Caddy does and compares the doors of
# every shop hostname against every other, so a door added to one and not the rest is red the same day.
#
#   {args[0]} — the compose service serving the VITRINE at this hostname (`storefront`, or a fork of it).
(forge_shop_host) {
	# Who may reach the control face. Empty is not something `remote_ip` accepts, so "nobody" is written as
	# loopback — a source address no request arriving over the internet can have. Put your VPN / bastion CIDRs
	# in FORGE_CONTROL_ALLOW_CIDR (space-separated) if you want it reachable, and leave it alone otherwise.
	@control_allowed remote_ip {$FORGE_CONTROL_ALLOW_CIDR:127.0.0.1/32}
	handle /v1/control/* {
		route {
			reverse_proxy @control_allowed kernel:3000
			respond "the control face is not exposed on this interface" 403
		}
	}
	handle /v1/* {
		reverse_proxy kernel:3000
	}
	# ★ SURFACES-EXT S2 — THE REMOTE MCP ENDPOINT, AND IT IS ITS OWN PATH ON PURPOSE. MCP negotiates its
	# version in-band (the `MCP-Protocol-Version` header), so the route carries none — which is exactly why it
	# does not live under /v1/* and has to be named here. It was NOT, and that is the defect this block closes:
	# S2 mounted it in the kernel, S3 drew the edge, and the seam between the two slices had no owner. The
	# comment at the top of this file already promised "an MCP agent" among the callers reaching the kernel,
	# so the file was asserting a route it did not carry. Measured on Staging 2026-08-24: POST /mcp answered
	# 405 from the STOREFRONT (Next refusing a method on a page that is not there), never reaching the kernel,
	# while /health and /v1/read/internal/* both landed correctly. ★ AND IT IS IN THE SNIPPET RATHER THAN ON
	# ONE HOST: which STORE an agent buys from is the Host it connected to, so an endpoint that covered the
	# primary hostname only would serve the promise on the one hostname multistore does not use.
	handle /mcp {
		reverse_proxy kernel:3000
	}
	handle /webhooks/payment/* {
		reverse_proxy kernel:3000
	}
	# ★★ QA17/FM3 — THE CHANNEL CALLBACK SEAM, and it was missing while the admin was already telling
	# merchants to paste its address into their provider's console. The provider POSTs delivery receipts here
	# (apps/api/src/channel-webhook-adapter.ts, keyed on the INSTALLATION so two merchants on one box hold two
	# different addresses). Without this block the request fell through to the storefront and came back as its
	# 404 — an integration that can only fail, from an address a screen handed out.
	handle /webhooks/channel/* {
		reverse_proxy kernel:3000
	}
	handle /health {
		reverse_proxy kernel:3000
	}
	# ★★ CHECKOUT-APP (C3) — THE SECOND FRONT ON THIS HOST, AND THE ONLY THING THAT PUTS IT THERE.
	#
	# `apps/checkout` is a deployable of its own (checkout, account, login, orders); `apps/storefront` is the
	# vitrine a customer forks. They share ONE hostname and split by PATH — never by subdomain, because a
	# subdomain would put `forge_cart` and `forge_customer_session` on another origin and the shopper would
	# lose the cart on the way to paying for it. These blocks ARE that split; without them the checkout
	# container is running and unreachable, and every address below answers the vitrine's 404.
	#
	# Declared in packages/codegen/src/control-exposure.ts (EDGE_HANDLES) in the Forge monorepo, which compares
	# that declaration against the Caddyfiles IT ships in both directions — this instance's copy is its own
	# file and is graded here, by `bin/box-domains.guard.mjs`, against the hostnames `seed/box.json` declares.
	#
	# ⚠️ ORDER IS NOT WHAT DECIDES THIS. Caddy matches `handle` blocks by how SPECIFIC their path is, not by
	# where they appear, so these win over the bare fall-through below wherever they are written. They are
	# written here because a reader needs to see them before the fall-through, not because Caddy does.
	handle /checkout* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	handle /account* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# ⚠️ `/api/account/orders/*` AND NOT `/api/account*`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A MEASURED ONE.
	# `/api/account/status` is STILL the vitrine's route (apps/storefront/src/app/api/account/status/route.ts)
	# — the header asks it after hydration to choose between "Entrar" and the shopper's name, and reading a
	# session is exactly what the vitrine is allowed to do. Widen this to the whole prefix and that fetch
	# lands on a deployable that does not serve it: the header is permanently signed out, the 404 happens
	# where no human is looking, and the account page itself still works, so nobody reports it.
	handle /api/account/orders/* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# RFC 8615: the whole prefix is host-level, never per-store. It is the CHECKOUT's because the social login
	# is — Apple fetches `apple-developer-domain-association.txt` at the bare host before it will trust the
	# Services ID whose handshake starts at `/account/social/<provider>/start`.
	handle /.well-known/* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# ★★ THE ASSETS, AND THIS IS THE ONE THAT FAILS IN SILENCE. Two Next servers on one origin both ask the
	# browser for `/_next/static/…`; whichever container wins that namespace serves chunks the other build has
	# never heard of. `assetPrefix: '/_checkout'` (apps/checkout/next.config.mjs) moves this app's assets to
	# their own prefix, and this block is the half at the edge. Without it the shopper meets an UNSTYLED
	# checkout holding their card number: the HTML arrives, no server logs anything, and the only trace is a
	# 404 for a stylesheet nobody was watching.
	#
	# ⚠️⚠️ `handle_path`, NOT `handle`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS SIX CHARACTERS AND THE WHOLE THING. `handle_path`
	# STRIPS the matched prefix before proxying. MEASURED on the real image (C3): a Next server with
	# `assetPrefix: '/_checkout'` emits the prefixed URL into its HTML and then answers **404** to it — it
	# serves those files at `/_next/static/…` and nothing else. The prefix is an instruction to THIS FILE, not
	# a route the app owns. With a bare `handle` every chunk reaches the right container and comes back 404,
	# which is the same unstyled checkout the prefix exists to prevent, one layer further in.
	handle_path /_checkout/* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# ★★ THE GENERATED ROUTES OF THE APPS WHOSE SLOTS LIVE HERE (slice C2, spec §2.3b). `forge.wiring.publicRoutes`
	# lets an app declare a public URL, and the composition writes it into the consumer where that app's SLOTS
	# are: `reviews` draws "Avaliar produtos" in the `order.*` slots, so `/apps/reviews/submit` is generated
	# into apps/checkout — and it asks for account-class injections, which is why it may not live in the
	# deployable a customer forks. Without this block the form POSTs to the vitrine, which does not serve it:
	# a shopper writes a review of something they bought and gets a 404 on submit.
	#
	# ⚠️ WHOLE-PREFIX, AND WHAT MAKES THAT SAFE IS A FACT ABOUT TODAY'S COMPOSITION. The one generated app
	# route that stays with the vitrine is the feed's, and it is `/feeds/google.xml` — outside this prefix. An
	# app whose slots live in the vitrine and that declared a route under `/apps/` would be silently taken by
	# this block.
	handle /apps/* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# ★ THE CHECKOUT'S COMPONENT GALLERY (slice C4), the sibling of the vitrine's `/ui-storefront`. That one
	# needs no block because the vitrine is the fall-through; this one is on the other side of the split.
	handle /ui-checkout* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# ★ WHO SERVED. Set by the ROUTE, so a reader can prove which front the edge chose instead of inferring it
	# from a theme attribute — which proves the THEME and misled two people into believing the café's fork was
	# serving a page the vanilla front was rendering. `bin/prove-doors.mjs` grades this header.
	handle {
		header X-Forge-Served-By "{args[0]}"
		reverse_proxy {args[0]}:3000
	}
}

# ── THE SHOPS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One site block per store hostname `seed/box.json` declares. The argument is the container whose VITRINE
# answers there; everything else about the hostname is the snippet above.

{$FORGE_DOMAIN:store.unset.localhost} {
	# Certificates you were GIVEN, rather than ones Caddy issues. If a CDN sits in front of this box in
	# full-strict mode, it terminates TLS and the ACME challenge can never reach here — so automatic
	# renewal cannot work and the certificate expires ~90 days after it was issued. The way out is the
	# CDN's Origin Certificate: `cat origin.crt origin.key > caddy/certs/origin.pem` and restart.
	#
	# An EMPTY folder is the normal state and changes nothing — Caddy keeps issuing its own certificates.
	# ★ ONE DECLARATION COVERS ALL SIX HOSTNAMES: the certificate pool is process-wide, not per-site. It is
	# written on this block because this is the box's root shop, not because this block is special.
	tls {
		load /etc/caddy/certs
	}
	import forge_shop_host storefront
}

# The outlet: the SAME two containers as the root shop, on a hostname of its own. Nothing about this store is
# a fork — its whole thesis is that a theme plus data plus composition is enough — so what makes this address
# the outlet is only which store the kernel's directory says answers here.
{$FORGE_OUTLET_DOMAIN:outlet.unset.localhost} {
	import forge_shop_host storefront
}

# ★★ THE COFFEE SHOP, AND IT IS THE ONE HOSTNAME WHOSE VITRINE IS NOT OURS. `storefront-coffee` is a fork
# this repository owns and builds into an image of its own; its checkout, account and sign-in stay with the
# checkout we host, exactly like every other shop's. That is the customisation posture this demo exists to
# show, at an address.
{$FORGE_CAFE_DOMAIN:cafe.unset.localhost} {
	import forge_shop_host storefront-coffee
	# ⚠️⚠️ THE ONE DOOR THIS HOSTNAME NEEDS AND ITS SIBLINGS DO NOT, AND ITS ABSENCE IS SILENT. The fork
	# carries `assetPrefix: '/_coffee'` in its image (storefront-coffee/next.config.mjs) — unconditionally,
	# so it emits `/_coffee/_next/…` into its HTML on EVERY hostname it is served at, including this one where
	# it has the origin to itself, and it answers 404 to that URL: it serves those files at `/_next/static/…`
	# and nowhere else. `handle_path` (never `handle`) strips the prefix. Without this block the coffee shop
	# is served UNSTYLED, the HTML arrives, no server logs anything, and the only trace is a 404 for a
	# stylesheet nobody was watching — the same defect the checkout paid for in C3.
	handle_path /_coffee/* {
		header X-Forge-Served-By "storefront-coffee"
		reverse_proxy storefront-coffee:3000
	}
}

# ── THE ADMINS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ★★ TWO HOSTNAMES, ONE CONTAINER, TWO TENANTS. With FORGE_ADMIN_TENANT empty the admin runs in HOST mode and
# asks the kernel which tenant the request's `Host` belongs to (`read.admin.by_host`); `platform.admin_host.set`
# writes that map. So a second brand costs a hostname, not a second container — and each admin is its own
# origin, so the session cookie of one is not the session cookie of the other, which is what stops an operator
# acting on one tenant while believing they are on the other.
#
# ⚠️ THE SECOND ONE USED TO BE A FILE IN `caddy/extra/`, HAND-WRITTEN PER BOX. That folder's recipe is still
# right for a THIRD tenant somebody adds to one deployment — but a face EVERY deployment of this instance has
# is not an extra, and a hostname that only exists in an untracked file is a hostname no guard can grade.

{$FORGE_ADMIN_DOMAIN:admin.unset.localhost} {
	reverse_proxy admin:3000
}

{$FORGE_CAFE_ADMIN_DOMAIN:admin-cafe.unset.localhost} {
	reverse_proxy admin:3000
}

# ── THE COUNTER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ★★ THE COUNTER'S TOTEM (T2·S2), on a hostname of its own — and it is a WHOLE SITE with no path split,
# which is the entire architectural claim of that slice: the totem is ONE app of the client's own serving
# EVERY path of this host. There is no second Next server behind it, so there is nothing to divide — no
# `/checkout*` to peel off, no `/_checkout/*` assets, no store in the URL. It resolves its store from its
# own environment (`FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_ID`), which is why this block needs no store name either.
#
# ⚠️ AND THE STORE BEHIND IT DECLARES `directory: false` IN `seed/box.json`, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT FACT FROM
# THIS BLOCK. This block says a front of this box answers at that hostname. The declaration says the KERNEL's
# address book must NOT claim it for the counter's store — because the kernel writes `https://<host>` into
# every transactional message and the totem serves ONE route, so a `host` on that store would put an
# «Acompanhar o pedido» button on every counter receipt pointing at the totem's own 404. The two halves are
# graded separately: `bin/box-domains.guard.mjs` grades this one, `bin/prove-doors.mjs` grades the other.
#
# The certificate comes from the `tls` declaration in the first shop block: the pool is process-wide, not
# per-site. The kernel's own faces are NOT routed here on purpose — the totem talks to the kernel
# server-side over the compose network, and a till has no reason to expose a command face to a room.
{$FORGE_TOTEM_DOMAIN:totem.unset.localhost} {
	reverse_proxy totem:3000
}

# Hostnames beyond the six — a THIRD tenant's admin, a second brand this box grows later. They go in
# caddy/extra/, one file each, instead of here: a hostname every deployment of this instance has belongs in
# this file where a guard can see it, and a hostname one BOX happens to serve does not. An empty folder is
# the normal state: Caddy accepts a pattern that matches nothing (one `warn` line in the log and it carries
# on), so leaving it empty changes nothing at all.
#
# A hostname added there behaves like the ones above — Caddy matches site blocks by how specific they are,
# not by where they appear — and gets its certificate automatically. See caddy/extra/README.md for the whole
# recipe (the container, the hostname, the reload).
import /etc/caddy/extra/*.caddy
